{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8847d22fe6697e73","type":"tool_result","content":"# Component: eval-hub\n\n## Metadata\n\n- **Repository**: https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/eval-hub.git\n- **Version**: d043d23f6336f41e7508137fed1c46b52a65ecf6\n- **Distribution**: RHOAI\n- **Languages**: Go\n- **Deployment Type**: Application Service\n- **Generated By**: Claude Opus 4.6 on 2026-08-13\n\n## Purpose\n\n**Short**: Eval-hub is an evaluation orchestration service for RHOAI that manages evaluation jobs, collections, and providers through a REST API, coordinating with Kubernetes batch Jobs, MLflow tracking, S3-compatible storage, and OpenTelemetry for observability. [source: internal/eval_hub/server/server.go:444, internal/eval_hub/mlflow/mlflow.go:24, cmd/eval_runtime_init/main.go:114, internal/otel/otel_sdk.go:174]\n\n**Detailed**: Eval-hub provides a centralized evaluation orchestration service for Red Hat OpenShift AI. The primary server (`eval_hub`) exposes a REST API on port 8080 with endpoints for managing evaluation jobs, collections, and providers, protected by conditional identity header enforcement (X-User/X-Tenant) when enabled by configuration. A dedicated Prometheus metrics server runs on port 8081 in cluster mode. The service coordinates evaluation workloads by creating Kubernetes batch Jobs, ConfigMaps, and Secrets via client-go, and manages HardwareProfile custom resources for hardware-aware scheduling. It integrates with MLflow for experiment tracking via a TLS-configured HTTP client, S3-compatible storage for test data via AWS SDK, and exports traces/metrics to an OpenTelemetry Collector over OTLP/gRPC. The `eval_runtime_sidecar` acts as a proxy for evaluation runtime containers, `eval_runtime_init` handles S3 test-data downloads as an init container, and `evalhub_mcp` provides a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server interface. All Go binaries are built with `CGO_ENABLED=1` and `GOEXPERIMENT=strictfipsruntime` for FIPS compliance on UBI9. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:27-52, internal/eval_hub/server/server.go:444, internal/eval_hub/config/prometheus.go:4, internal/eval_hub/runtimes/k8s/k8s_helper.go:112-232]\n\n## Architectural Analysis\n\nEval-hub follows a multi-process architecture where the main API server orchestrates evaluation workloads through Kubernetes batch primitives. The `setupRoutes` function at `server.go:444` registers route groups for evaluation jobs, collections, providers, health, and documentation, all behind a conditional identity enforcement middleware that checks `X-User` and `X-Tenant` headers when the `RequiresIdentityHeaders()` configuration flag is set—bypassed entirely in local/development mode (`server.go:430-441`). This configuration-gated authentication model means that identity enforcement depends on the deployment configuration rather than being structurally guaranteed, which is a meaningful security consideration for platform operators.\n\nThe Kubernetes interaction layer in `KubernetesHelper` (`k8s_helper.go`) provides CRUD operations across ConfigMaps, Secrets, Jobs, and HardwareProfile custom resources (`infrastructure.opendatahub.io/v1`). Evaluation workloads are dispatched as Kubernetes batch Jobs, with ConfigMaps carrying evaluation configurations and Secrets holding credentials. The HardwareProfile CRD integration enables hardware-aware scheduling of evaluation workloads.\n\nThe service maintains optional integrations with MLflow for experiment tracking (`mlflow.go:43-92`), constructing an HTTP client with TLS 1.2-1.3 enforcement and optional custom CA certificate loading. The MLflow client is nil-safe—when no tracking URI is configured, the server starts without MLflow integration. Storage uses pluggable SQL backends (SQLite for local development, PostgreSQL for production), with the database schema managed through the `internal/eval_hub/storage/sql` package.\n\nNo Kubernetes Service or ingress manifests exist in this repository; service exposure is platform-delegated, consistent with the `io.openshift.expose-services=\"\"` label in the Dockerfile. The metrics server runs on a separate port (default 8081) in cluster mode, while in local mode `/metrics` is also served on the main API port for development convenience (`server.go:470-477`).\n\n## Provenance\n\n### Repo Lineage\n\n| Role | Repository | Sync Mechanism | Sync Branch | Sync Workflows | Detection Method |\n|----|----------|--------------|-----------|--------------|----------------|\n| Upstream | https://github.com/opendatahub-io/eval-hub | auto_merge | main | -- | sync_config |\n| Downstream | https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/eval-hub | auto_merge | main | `check-trustyai-service-operator-configmap-sync.yml`, `sync-branch-incubation.yaml`, `sync-branch-stable.yaml` | local_analysis |\n\n### Aliases\n\n| Current Name | Previous Name | Type | Context |\n|------------|-------------|----|-------|\n\n## Architecture Components\n\n| Component | Type | Purpose |\n|---------|----|-------|\n| Dockerfile.konflux:CMD | Container entrypoint | [\"/app/eval-hub\"] |\n| Metrics Server | HTTP Service | Standalone Prometheus metrics service backed by a runtime HTTP listener |\n| eval_hub | Go executable | cmd/eval_hub |\n| eval_runtime_init | Go executable | cmd/eval_runtime_init |\n| eval_runtime_sidecar | Go executable | cmd/eval_runtime_sidecar |\n| evalhub_mcp | Go executable | cmd/evalhub_mcp |\n| validate_configs | Go executable | cmd/validate_configs |\n\n## APIs Exposed\n\n### Custom Resource Definitions (CRDs)\n\n| Group | Version | Kind | Scope | API Role | Purpose |\n|-----|-------|----|-----|--------|-------|\n\n----\n# Component: odh-dashboard\n\n## Metadata\n\n- **Repository**: https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/odh-dashboard.git\n- **Version**: 04cfb8c815012a4a373ebe201dccc3ea83821c31\n- **Distribution**: RHOAI\n- **Languages**: Go, TypeScript\n- **Deployment Type**: Kubernetes Operator / Controller + Sidecar utilities\n- **Generated By**: Claude Opus 4.6\n\n## Purpose\n\n**Short**: odh-dashboard provides the web-based management console for Red Hat OpenShift AI, combining a React/PatternFly frontend with a Go operator and ten federated Go BFF sidecars to present a unified UI for managing notebooks, model serving, model registry, experiment tracking, and AI agent operations. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:52, Dockerfile.konflux.dashboard-operator:37, dashboard-operator/charts/dashboard/values.yaml:18]\n\n**Detailed**: odh-dashboard is a multi-component platform console consisting of three tiers: (1) a React/PatternFly single-page application served via Webpack Module Federation that hosts domain-specific UI modules; (2) a Node.js Fastify backend that proxies Kubernetes API requests using user impersonation and serves static assets behind kube-rbac-proxy; and (3) a Go operator (dashboard-operator) built on controller-runtime v0.23.3 that reconciles Dashboard custom resources, managing the lifecycle of the console deployment including RBAC, routes, and sidecar BFF services. The federated architecture includes ten Go BFF (backend-for-frontend) sidecars — core-bff, gen-ai, model-registry, mlflow, eval-hub, maas, agent-ops, automl, autorag, and data-registry — each serving domain-specific API endpoints on dedicated TLS-encrypted ports with Bearer token authentication. The operator watches platform CRDs (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, KServe InferenceService, ModelRegistry, MLflow, TrustyAI, Feast) to reflect platform state in the dashboard and manages Gateway API HTTPRoutes for request routing. A bundled Workspace controller reconciles kubeflow.org/v1beta1 Workspace and WorkspaceKind resources with validating and conversion webhooks. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:52, Dockerfile.konflux.dashboard-operator:37, dashboard-operator/charts/dashboard/values.yaml:15-18, manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, dashboard-operator/go.mod:6-13]\n\n## Architectural Analysis\n\nThe odh-dashboard follows a micro-frontend architecture using Webpack Module Federation, where a host React application dynamically loads domain-specific UI modules (gen-ai, model-registry, notebooks, eval-hub, agent-ops, automl, autorag, data-registry, maas, mlflow) at runtime via `/_mf/:name/*` paths. Each UI module is paired with a dedicated Go BFF sidecar that handles its backend operations, creating a vertically-sliced architecture where each domain owns its full request path from UI through BFF to Kubernetes API. The federation configuration (in `manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml`) maps path prefixes to sidecar ports, enabling a single ingress point to fan out to domain-specific backends. [source: manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, backend/src/routes/module-federation.ts:1]\n\nThe dashboard-operator reconciles Dashboard custom resources created by the rhods-operator, deploying and configuring the console pod alongside its BFF sidecars. The operator implements a declarative ownership model where the parent Dashboard CR controls the lifecycle of Deployments, Services, Routes, NetworkPolicies, PodDisruptionBudgets, and RBAC resources via controller-runtime's `Owns()` watches. Controller watches on platform CRDs (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, KServe InferenceService, ModelRegistry, MLflow, TrustyAI, Feast) enable the dashboard to reflect the real-time state of the AI platform without polling. The operator uses `odh-platform-utilities` for platform detection, manifest rendering, and deployment helpers. [source: dashboard-operator/charts/dashboard/values.yaml:15-18, dashboard-operator/go.mod:6, dashboard-operator/config/rbac/role.yaml:1]\n\nAuthentication follows a two-layer model: kube-rbac-proxy fronts the Node.js backend (port 8443) performing OpenShift OAuth validation and injecting identity headers (`x-forwarded-access-token`, `x-auth-request-user`, `x-auth-request-groups`), while each Go BFF sidecar independently validates Bearer tokens and performs SubjectAccessReviews against the Kubernetes API for fine-grained authorization. The agent-ops BFF extends this with per-agent RBAC via SubjectAccessReviews for agent-specific access control. The Node.js backend additionally proxies Kubernetes API calls (`/api/k8s/*`) by converting user tokens to impersonation headers, allowing the frontend to interact with the cluster API through the dashboard service boundary. [source: backend/src/utils/constants.ts:18-20, backend/src/utils/proxy.ts:39-42, packages/agent-ops/bff/internal/api/middleware.go:1]\n\nThe component packages a Workspace controller (reconciling kubeflow.org/v1beta1 Workspace and WorkspaceKind resources with validating and conversion webhooks) alongside the dashboard operator. Workspaces are managed via StatefulSets with associated Services and PodDisruptionBudgets. Cert-manager provisions TLS certificates for the webhook and metrics endpoints. All Go binaries (operator and BFF sidecars) are built with `CGO_ENABLED=1` and `-tags strictfipsruntime` for FIPS-compliant cryptographic operations through system OpenSSL. [source: Dockerfile.konflux.dashboard-operator:18-20, Dockerfile.konflux.genai:86, packages/notebooks/upstream/workspaces/controller/internal/webhook/workspace_webhook.go:44]\n\n## Provenance\n\n### Repo Lineage\n\n| Role | Repository | Sync Mechanism | Sync Branch | Sync Workflows | Detection Method |\n|----|----------|--------------|-----------|--------------|----------------|\n| Upstream | https://github.com/opendatahub-io/odh-dashboard | auto_merge | main | -- | sync_config |\n| Downstream | https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/odh-dashboard | auto_merge | main | `dependabot-auto-merge.yml`, `release-auto-merge.yml` | local_analysis |\n\n### Aliases\n\n| Current Name | Previous Name | Type | Context |\n|------------|-------------|----|-------|","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-af589b60cf509813","agent_id":"a76d34cd3e2df0381","session_id":"1