rrable features: E003's P1 HLR-6 (Page Visibility) is tightly coupled to the same polling-hook implementation surface as the P0 work, and HLR-4's fetch side is inseparable from E003's concurrency layer — both correctly kept bundled per the coupling rule. HLR-7 (P2) is a separate epic because its existence is gated by E002, which is the right reason (deferrable via gate, not arbitrary priority split). Triage consistency: `triage` is null (full decomposition) with epic_count 6 — no contradiction.\n41\t\n42\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n43\t\n44\tCross-checked every diagram edge against epic frontmatter `dependencies`:\n45\t\n46\t- E001 → E003 (E003 deps: [E001]) ✓; E001 → E004 (E004 deps: [E001, E003]) ✓; E003 → E004 ✓; E002 → E006 (E006 deps: [E002, E005]) ✓; E005 → E006 ✓. The `E005 -. parallel .- E003` dotted link is an annotation, not a blocking edge — correctly not reflected in frontmatter. Arrows point dependency → dependent per convention. No cycles. No frontmatter dependency references a nonexistent epic.\n47\t\n48\tEvery blocking edge is justified: E001→E003/E004 and E002→E006 are Rule 3 investigation gates (true gates with documented failure impact); E003→E004 is Rule 12/13 framework→consumer (the display renders data delivered by the polling hook); E005→E006 is Rule 12 (failure distinction extends the state badge framework). Parallelism is correct: E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E005 (independent questions and independent list-endpoint UI work), E003 ∥ E005 (Rule 15, same repo, different areas). No konflux-onboarding/repo-onboarding/license-validation epics exist (both components have architecture context files — verified `eval-hub.md` and `odh-dashboard.md` in `.context/architecture-context/`), so rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are N/A.\n49\t\n50\tNo branch files exist; the decomposition explicitly documents the decision to model both bounded investigation outcomes via `gated_by` + `gate_failure_impact` instead — justified, because the negative outcomes are scope degradations of the same epics with strategy-prescribed fallbacks (rewrite/remove), not structurally distinct downstream epic sets. No main-plan epic references a branch node.\n51\t\n52\tMinor (informational): critical path length 3 (E001→E003→E004) slightly exceeds the S-size heuristic of 1-2. Acceptable here — the genuine-unknowns escape from triage is correctly applied (both \"needs validation\" open questions change downstream scope), and the E003/E004 split follows the architectural sub-system boundary (data-fetching layer vs. presentation), so the path length is inherent to the strategy rather than a serialization defect.\n53\t\n54\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n55\t\n56\tAll Implementation epics (E003-E006) are component \"AI Evaluations\" (canonical name verified in `.context/rhai-components.txt`), team ODH Dashboard — matching the strategy's single implementing team. The two Investigations (E001/E002) are owned by TrustyAI / EvalHub, matching the strategy's open-question ownership; the component/team boundary rule applies to Implementations, not Investigations, so this is correct. The passive odh-dashboard BFF component (\"no code change expected\") is correctly modeled as a verification AC on E003 rather than a separate epic — the same team (ODH Dashboard) owns both components, so the Step 2 passive-component exception for a different validating team does not apply.\n57\t\n58\tNo scope duplication: E003 builds the data-fetching hook, E004 the progress display, E005 the state badge, E006 the failure-state variant — each builds a distinct artifact; no epic independently rebuilds another's data structure or endpoint. No work-product type mixing (UX review appears only as review ACs, not content authoring). No epic exceeds ~2 weeks — the whole strategy is S-sized (1-2 sprints) and the largest epic (E003) is a single well-understood hook.\n59\t\n60\tMinor: E004's scope includes \"state-cell/table-row layout changes to present progress alongside the state badge\" while E005 renders the state badge in the same cell — both epics modify the EvaluationsTable row/state-cell surface in parallel with no coordination edge. Under Rule 15 this is coordination risk rather than a dependency, but the shared surface is a merge-conflict risk worth flagging to the planner.\n61\t\n62\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n63\t\n64\tBoth Investigations genuinely change downstream structure: E001's answer rewrites E003/E004 scope (mid-run benchmark availability determines whether detail polling yields progress data); E002's answer determines whether E006 exists at all. Both have downstream dependents. All Implementations produce concrete artifacts (hooks, UI rendering).\n65\t\n66\tGating metadata is complete and correct: E003 (`gated_by: E001`, action `rewrite`, fallback populated), E004 (`gated_by: E001`, `rewrite`, fallback populated), E006 (`gated_by: E002`, `remove`, fallback populated) — every `gated_by` has both `action` and `fallback_approach`, and every `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies` list. No epic lists an Investigation in `dependencies` without `gated_by`. E005 correctly has no gate (its data source is confirmed available). Fallback approaches match the strategy's documented risk mitigations verbatim.\n67\t\n68\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n69\t\n70\tSignal sets are type-correct: E001/E002 carry `investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric); E003-E006 carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric). All six ai-signals rationale files exist and their values match frontmatter exactly.\n71\t\n72\tInvestigation signals are consistent with the rubric: question_specificity +1 (both questions are concrete and enumerable with defined verdicts), source_accessibility +1 (open upstream repo + published API reference hold the answer), local_runnability 0 (the server runs locally per eval-hub.md but a running evaluation job dispatches as a K8s Job, so the deciding observation can't be exercised purely locally), cluster_hardware_dependence -1 (cluster needed only for peripheral empirical confirmation, not the gating answer), human_judgment_required 0 (technical facts, no stakeholder decision).\n73\t\n74\tImplementation signals are consistent with strategy content: E003's open_questions -1 (gated on E001) and human_process_gates -1 (Architecture Review and Performance Team Support pending) are justified; E006's change_specificity 0 (triggering message_code values unknown pending the catalog) is exactly right; E005's open_questions +1 is defensible — the strategy marks the `status.state` availability assumption as confirmed and the genuinely unknown piece is scoped to the separately gated E006. E004/E005/E006 adapter_pattern 0 (design-system convention, no single reference implementation) is appropriate. Arithmetic/thresholds not checked per instructions.\n75\t\n76\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n77\t\n78\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy's acceptance criteria: all 7 strategy ACs are allocated (AC1/AC3 → E004; AC2/AC4 → E005; AC5 → E006; AC6/AC7 → E003), plus implementation-specific ACs (5-concurrency cap, visible-page-only polling, BFF passthrough verification, graceful degradation, list-polling coexistence, single-benchmark fallback). ACs are measurable (Network-tab verification, count-matching rules, sign-off).\n79\t\n80\tRule-mandated ACs: no replacement of existing capability (purely additive — rollback/feature-flag N/A), no docs-authoring epic (Documentation Support is \"_Pending human review_\", not \"Yes\", and no substantive content authoring is in scope), no konflux chain (components already in platform — build pipeline AC N/A). No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID — dependency prose uses epic names, and E-number strings appear only in frontmatter where they are structural.\n81\t\n82\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n83\t\n84\tFull scope coverage: two-layer polling, 10s/round-robin/5-cap concurrency, retry-with-backoff with placeholder data, inline warning, Page Visibility pause/resume, progress model (X/N + elapsed time with `resource.created_at` fallback), state differentiation, pre-start/runtime failure distinction, BFF passthrough verification (passive component as AC), auth-chain constraint (no new paths), and all three NFRs (performance caps, security, backwards compatibility) appear in epic scope/ACs.\n85\t\n86\tStrategy-level context is carried forward, not dropped: both open questions became the two Investigations with the strategy's owners; all four risk mitigations appear as epic ACs or documented gate fallbacks (single-benchmark elapsed-time-primary → E004 AC; single-Failed-state fallback → E006 AC; scalability deferral noted in the summary; 5xx-simulation validation reflected in E003's error-path ACs); UXD support requirement carried as review ACs on E004/E005/E006; pending human gates surfaced as health warnings and in human_process_gates scoring. Conditional outcomes are covered on both paths — each gate's `fallback_approach` documents the negative outcome per the strategy's own risk table. Out-of-scope items (server API extensions, notifications, log access, push infrastructure) are correctly excluded from the epic set.\n87\t\n88\tMinor: the strategy's risk-table mitigation \"validate in development by simulating 5xx errors from the eval-hub-ui BFF on individual detail endpoints\" is reflected behaviorally in E003's error-path ACs but the simulation-based test setup is not an explicit AC — a completeness nit, not a gap.\n89\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9fb63b737b075928","agent_id":"a4271d12c344d366e","session_id":"1da74765-444a-4960-9d12-3d9467f39461","uuid":"973e7ea1-3833-4308-adc3-4c0edd4ceefb","timestamp":"2026-10-22T09:03:13.446Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-935922c5fbfabc73","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8005 decomposition"}
